[https://github.com/SoleSoul/owbox owbox] is a stacking [[Wayland]] compositor based on the code of [[Openbox]]. It reads Openbox's configuration files ({{ic|rc.xml}}, {{ic|menu.xml}}) and themes, and keeps Openbox's window management behavior, on top of wlroots. It is an independent project, not Openbox, and is not affiliated with the Openbox developers.

== Installation ==

[[Install]] the {{AUR|owbox}} package, or {{AUR|owbox-git}} for the development version.

Optional dependencies add the parts of a desktop that owbox does not draw itself, see [[#A complete desktop]]. For X applications install {{Pkg|xorg-xwayland}}. For the settings application, ''owbox Settings'', install {{Pkg|python-gobject}} and {{Pkg|gtk4}}.

== Starting ==

Choose the ''owbox'' session in a [[display manager]] that supports Wayland sessions, or run {{ic|owbox-session}} from a [[Linux console|virtual console]]. {{ic|owbox-session}} reads {{ic|~/.config/owbox/environment}} first, like ''openbox-session'' does, and points {{ic|SSH_AUTH_SOCK}} at the [[SSH keys#SSH agents|SSH agent]] started by systemd.

Running {{ic|owbox}} inside another Wayland or X session opens it in a window, which is useful for trying it out.

owbox starts the [[systemd/User|user]] unit {{ic|owbox-session.target}}, which is bound to {{ic|graphical-session.target}}, so user services that belong to the graphical session (for example {{ic|waybar.service}}) start and stop with it.

== Configuration ==

=== owbox Settings ===

Run {{ic|owbox-settings}} (also in owbox's root menu). It covers the theme, the look of applications (GTK theme, icons, cursor, fonts, light or dark), window behavior, monitors, desktops, keyboard layouts, mouse and touchpad, key bindings, idle and locking, and autostart. Changes are saved to {{ic|rc.xml}} and applied immediately.

=== Configuration files ===

owbox looks for each file in {{ic|~/.config/owbox/}}, then {{ic|~/.config/openbox/}}, then {{ic|/etc/xdg/owbox/}}, so an existing Openbox configuration is used as it is. To give owbox its own copy:

 $ mkdir -p ~/.config/owbox
 $ cp /etc/xdg/owbox/{rc.xml,menu.xml,autostart,environment} ~/.config/owbox/

{{ic|rc.xml}} is reloaded when it is saved. Settings that Openbox does not have go in an {{ic|<owbox>}} section, which Openbox ignores: keyboard layouts, input devices, monitors, cursor and icon themes, snapping, idle and locking, the size of owbox's decorations ({{ic|<scale>}}), and panels. The installed {{ic|/etc/xdg/owbox/rc.xml}} documents each of them, and the {{ic|owbox(1)}} man page describes the rest.

=== Autostart ===

Programs to run at login go in {{ic|~/.config/owbox/autostart}}, a shell script. Applications in the [[XDG Autostart]] directories are started too. owbox does not run {{ic|~/.config/openbox/autostart}}, because it usually starts X programs that have Wayland replacements; owbox Settings' ''Autostart'' page lists its commands with what replaces each one.

=== Migrating from Openbox ===

* Key bindings, mouse bindings, menus, per-application settings and themes keep working.
* X tools in an Openbox setup need Wayland replacements: a panel such as ''tint2'' becomes [[Waybar]], ''nitrogen'' or ''feh'' becomes {{Pkg|swaybg}}, ''setxkbmap'' and ''xinput'' become owbox Settings' keyboard and mouse pages, ''xrandr'' its displays page, ''xscreensaver'' and ''xset'' are replaced by owbox's idle settings with {{Pkg|swaylock}}.
* owbox's own default key bindings (media keys, Print Screen, tapping Super) are not in an Openbox {{ic|rc.xml}}; owbox Settings' ''Key Bindings'' page offers to add them.
* ''obconf'' is replaced by owbox Settings.

== A complete desktop ==

owbox manages windows, draws their frames, menus and the Alt+Tab switcher, turns the monitors off when idle, and binds the media keys and Print Screen. The rest of a desktop is up to other programs. These fit owbox best; owbox also works with any alternative that supports the same Wayland protocols.

